[[:db/retract 17592186134375 :question/answer-fields 17592186132289] [:db/retract 17592186134367 :question/answer-fields 17592186132241] {:db/id 17592186133882 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dfcce989-6529-57a3-8788-c6f5ffffe36a" "question-581c6229-8cb3-5361-87bc-faf26b91b942" "question-25b5bd80-ef51-5481-b2fb-7d3f90359e46" "question-4af8cfd2-bdac-5f91-b83d-a441b5fa79ce" "question-631300c6-4369-57db-a4e4-4ed4d7169680"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133884 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-196f72ff-1ff2-5e8e-8905-6141cb15ad66" "question-0fd75856-9255-5679-88e6-6aeda24c9a6c" "question-c14fef09-0118-528c-b9c9-91bf74ebf7eb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133883 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5bd170e0-be10-5a08-9034-fb1371f7f685" "question-6fbdc55e-ae46-5858-9840-dcfa0a36807f" "question-8c2f5a35-b3df-565f-bebb-8a3475dfd0d6" "question-4383616d-c261-5116-9385-9c929fedb95e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-dfcce989-6529-57a3-8788-c6f5ffffe36a" :question/id #uuid "dfcce989-6529-57a3-8788-c6f5ffffe36a" :question/math-academy-id "q-108830" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(2,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bec8a0243d2ea9e983cab4c07719ff4a5d309960039ccdd77e621fd15ffd7ed.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}y+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b75894da-7bf9-54c7-ab7b-ac317b6f0581"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b75894da-7bf9-54c7-ab7b-ac317b6f0581" :answer-field/id #uuid "b75894da-7bf9-54c7-ab7b-ac317b6f0581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1557707f-89c6-567b-bb30-f8c64368ff64" :answer-field/choices ["answer-af98450c-dfab-596e-b54e-440b639e4538" "answer-3cf53b6c-fab4-56c1-9e1c-7866457b5b5f" "answer-1557707f-89c6-567b-bb30-f8c64368ff64" "answer-681da06e-fe7e-503f-ae8f-8cbde680fd97" "answer-73c8af1d-00e0-5e51-be53-dabd553965d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af98450c-dfab-596e-b54e-440b639e4538" :answer/id #uuid "af98450c-dfab-596e-b54e-440b639e4538" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{x}^{2}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cf53b6c-fab4-56c1-9e1c-7866457b5b5f" :answer/id #uuid "3cf53b6c-fab4-56c1-9e1c-7866457b5b5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1557707f-89c6-567b-bb30-f8c64368ff64" :answer/id #uuid "1557707f-89c6-567b-bb30-f8c64368ff64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-681da06e-fe7e-503f-ae8f-8cbde680fd97" :answer/id #uuid "681da06e-fe7e-503f-ae8f-8cbde680fd97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73c8af1d-00e0-5e51-be53-dabd553965d9" :answer/id #uuid "73c8af1d-00e0-5e51-be53-dabd553965d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-581c6229-8cb3-5361-87bc-faf26b91b942" :question/id #uuid "581c6229-8cb3-5361-87bc-faf26b91b942" :question/math-academy-id "q-109129" :question/problem "Consider the path $C,$ in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the $x$-axis, for $y\\ge 0,$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dx+{x}^{2}y{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0d50e8f2faf679e6dee1471c8fa1ca2be4194796e606fc3e89372f0fefe8cfc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{e}^{-{y}^{2}}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dx+{x}^{2}y{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}-(-2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}})]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}+2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fbfb1dc9-ac7f-533d-9966-b616b6bc9a03"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fbfb1dc9-ac7f-533d-9966-b616b6bc9a03" :answer-field/id #uuid "fbfb1dc9-ac7f-533d-9966-b616b6bc9a03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1373e9bd-bdc4-5ee5-9dd4-483158ca8fbb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6d060811-a883-579d-9212-b1daa25ef195" "answer-4388dab3-b038-5c1c-837a-428bf31f6759" "answer-c01a8e02-f4d6-5df0-9c30-c6f58e29d206" "answer-1373e9bd-bdc4-5ee5-9dd4-483158ca8fbb" "answer-6b66bdb8-a403-5ed8-a631-d9248e4a923d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d060811-a883-579d-9212-b1daa25ef195" :answer/id #uuid "6d060811-a883-579d-9212-b1daa25ef195" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1+{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}){e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4388dab3-b038-5c1c-837a-428bf31f6759" :answer/id #uuid "4388dab3-b038-5c1c-837a-428bf31f6759" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c01a8e02-f4d6-5df0-9c30-c6f58e29d206" :answer/id #uuid "c01a8e02-f4d6-5df0-9c30-c6f58e29d206" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1373e9bd-bdc4-5ee5-9dd4-483158ca8fbb" :answer/id #uuid "1373e9bd-bdc4-5ee5-9dd4-483158ca8fbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b66bdb8-a403-5ed8-a631-d9248e4a923d" :answer/id #uuid "6b66bdb8-a403-5ed8-a631-d9248e4a923d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-25b5bd80-ef51-5481-b2fb-7d3f90359e46" :question/id #uuid "25b5bd80-ef51-5481-b2fb-7d3f90359e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-109196" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,1),(0,-1),(1,-1)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3e81cfacc47dfa241a5fea64de84e375a58a6d86ceab82c86303763d5e41fe5.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-(-2y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8258478b-1136-5626-9c52-3604e6dc2cb6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8258478b-1136-5626-9c52-3604e6dc2cb6" :answer-field/id #uuid "8258478b-1136-5626-9c52-3604e6dc2cb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-48f5574c-5c8a-571f-a5ec-a59c2f65571f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-67711d7b-91ec-5a44-89a3-3e9703cd9cc6" "answer-547db7e0-1aff-5b66-8afe-be02f76901b6" "answer-43fe0455-2f42-5377-bc8e-ce440159c478" "answer-48f5574c-5c8a-571f-a5ec-a59c2f65571f" "answer-7c5507aa-5a07-5e59-afae-c1b249eef1f6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67711d7b-91ec-5a44-89a3-3e9703cd9cc6" :answer/id #uuid "67711d7b-91ec-5a44-89a3-3e9703cd9cc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-547db7e0-1aff-5b66-8afe-be02f76901b6" :answer/id #uuid "547db7e0-1aff-5b66-8afe-be02f76901b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43fe0455-2f42-5377-bc8e-ce440159c478" :answer/id #uuid "43fe0455-2f42-5377-bc8e-ce440159c478" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48f5574c-5c8a-571f-a5ec-a59c2f65571f" :answer/id #uuid "48f5574c-5c8a-571f-a5ec-a59c2f65571f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c5507aa-5a07-5e59-afae-c1b249eef1f6" :answer/id #uuid "7c5507aa-5a07-5e59-afae-c1b249eef1f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4af8cfd2-bdac-5f91-b83d-a441b5fa79ce" :question/id #uuid "4af8cfd2-bdac-5f91-b83d-a441b5fa79ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-109199" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(0,-1)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)\\,dx+2{sin}^{2}⁡(xy)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f4bb6636c104cf17bcd4b25037a3490ba30b7c1380b152c33504d9015903bdca.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)$ and $Q(x,y)=2{sin}^{2}⁡xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)\\,dx+2{sin}^{2}⁡(xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)-(-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-edcf7bc8-f757-5f28-b389-f3fbae1ad417"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-edcf7bc8-f757-5f28-b389-f3fbae1ad417" :answer-field/id #uuid "edcf7bc8-f757-5f28-b389-f3fbae1ad417" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c264dd4e-c055-5e7a-bc5a-8d050645e7d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5ca40ef0-e014-586e-b378-13e06e23ad16" "answer-c264dd4e-c055-5e7a-bc5a-8d050645e7d1" "answer-d468b014-e971-5635-97c8-eac3fe909b27" "answer-8f26a8d9-946b-54f3-9dd0-a27e7daa977e" "answer-b1502ceb-1419-5afd-b3f7-e9e6c6df5787"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ca40ef0-e014-586e-b378-13e06e23ad16" :answer/id #uuid "5ca40ef0-e014-586e-b378-13e06e23ad16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+2y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c264dd4e-c055-5e7a-bc5a-8d050645e7d1" :answer/id #uuid "c264dd4e-c055-5e7a-bc5a-8d050645e7d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d468b014-e971-5635-97c8-eac3fe909b27" :answer/id #uuid "d468b014-e971-5635-97c8-eac3fe909b27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f26a8d9-946b-54f3-9dd0-a27e7daa977e" :answer/id #uuid "8f26a8d9-946b-54f3-9dd0-a27e7daa977e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1502ceb-1419-5afd-b3f7-e9e6c6df5787" :answer/id #uuid "b1502ceb-1419-5afd-b3f7-e9e6c6df5787" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2y{cos}^{2}⁡(2xy)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-631300c6-4369-57db-a4e4-4ed4d7169680" :question/id #uuid "631300c6-4369-57db-a4e4-4ed4d7169680" :question/math-academy-id "q-109120" :question/problem "Consider the path $C,$ in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and the $y$-axis, for $x\\ge 0,$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}2{y}^{3/2}\\,dx+(x+2x\\sqrt{y})\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/93/93cb529eecb888a110eaad8f55ec458b3710f5a37b497aeed38fa32621178625.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2{y}^{3/2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+2x\\sqrt{y}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3\\sqrt{y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1+2\\sqrt{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}2{y}^{3/2}\\,dx+(x+2x\\sqrt{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+2\\sqrt{y}-3\\sqrt{y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-\\sqrt{y})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{y}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-daa742fd-2059-58a9-8c17-15a7e5c8ca78"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-daa742fd-2059-58a9-8c17-15a7e5c8ca78" :answer-field/id #uuid "daa742fd-2059-58a9-8c17-15a7e5c8ca78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-388b01e6-6a24-58b4-b0fe-57572ce8b425" :answer-field/choices ["answer-29edb23d-8a02-50c3-b830-5aa8eaaa3f21" "answer-388b01e6-6a24-58b4-b0fe-57572ce8b425" "answer-3b8fc7d9-f9fe-58d9-ada0-460b3e833c02" "answer-24b36ec1-7764-5048-a4d1-c122f6559c8f" "answer-f9106e33-884e-5c11-8c6d-9aed7d907a02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29edb23d-8a02-50c3-b830-5aa8eaaa3f21" :answer/id #uuid "29edb23d-8a02-50c3-b830-5aa8eaaa3f21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+2\\sqrt{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-388b01e6-6a24-58b4-b0fe-57572ce8b425" :answer/id #uuid "388b01e6-6a24-58b4-b0fe-57572ce8b425" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b8fc7d9-f9fe-58d9-ada0-460b3e833c02" :answer/id #uuid "3b8fc7d9-f9fe-58d9-ada0-460b3e833c02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24b36ec1-7764-5048-a4d1-c122f6559c8f" :answer/id #uuid "24b36ec1-7764-5048-a4d1-c122f6559c8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-\\sqrt{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9106e33-884e-5c11-8c6d-9aed7d907a02" :answer/id #uuid "f9106e33-884e-5c11-8c6d-9aed7d907a02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\sqrt{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-196f72ff-1ff2-5e8e-8905-6141cb15ad66" :question/id #uuid "196f72ff-1ff2-5e8e-8905-6141cb15ad66" :question/math-academy-id "q-109248" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the $y$-axis, for $x\\ge 0.$ Express ${∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-1)\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/92/92b9a6c83dd18a5599bbee71d714b26fddd62eb32917a5df983edc232664067b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55995149-0733-5aaa-8feb-5219bcb91ca4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55995149-0733-5aaa-8feb-5219bcb91ca4" :answer-field/id #uuid "55995149-0733-5aaa-8feb-5219bcb91ca4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3ea792f4-c1dd-5faa-a83b-af57c9e0e101" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f8bad17e-7a80-55fa-877b-8cf3e386ec15" "answer-3ea792f4-c1dd-5faa-a83b-af57c9e0e101" "answer-d8db4f17-8974-53ec-bf38-95643619fcaf" "answer-89af553e-248b-5c72-ab62-1d4e4315eda8" "answer-e1b0ae0b-92be-5756-b5b7-435352928f96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8bad17e-7a80-55fa-877b-8cf3e386ec15" :answer/id #uuid "f8bad17e-7a80-55fa-877b-8cf3e386ec15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ea792f4-c1dd-5faa-a83b-af57c9e0e101" :answer/id #uuid "3ea792f4-c1dd-5faa-a83b-af57c9e0e101" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8db4f17-8974-53ec-bf38-95643619fcaf" :answer/id #uuid "d8db4f17-8974-53ec-bf38-95643619fcaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89af553e-248b-5c72-ab62-1d4e4315eda8" :answer/id #uuid "89af553e-248b-5c72-ab62-1d4e4315eda8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1b0ae0b-92be-5756-b5b7-435352928f96" :answer/id #uuid "e1b0ae0b-92be-5756-b5b7-435352928f96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134375 :question/problem "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,3)$ and $(-1,3),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨3xy,{x}^{2}y⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6f831b2b707e63e8eb34ff5f6c1c45d0267f51001de2560834d212506b48ee41.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}3xy\\,dx+{x}^{2}y\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3xy$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}3xy\\,dx+{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(2y-3)\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9db11add-d8be-5410-9153-1e6c3098e557"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9db11add-d8be-5410-9153-1e6c3098e557" :answer-field/id #uuid "9db11add-d8be-5410-9153-1e6c3098e557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e3efbf16-6539-5ad4-bd13-0040d7d457e8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-03eefc0e-dc5f-50f8-8aa8-37fedc5b6795" "answer-e3efbf16-6539-5ad4-bd13-0040d7d457e8" "answer-d8a94d74-4321-5c41-9daa-93ace7652c67" "answer-eb384a4b-9a06-5ed5-bfb1-8be217c6cefa" "answer-4dfb517d-314f-5d0e-adde-8ffca8af7876"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03eefc0e-dc5f-50f8-8aa8-37fedc5b6795" :answer/id #uuid "03eefc0e-dc5f-50f8-8aa8-37fedc5b6795" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-3y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3efbf16-6539-5ad4-bd13-0040d7d457e8" :answer/id #uuid "e3efbf16-6539-5ad4-bd13-0040d7d457e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(2y-3)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8a94d74-4321-5c41-9daa-93ace7652c67" :answer/id #uuid "d8a94d74-4321-5c41-9daa-93ace7652c67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(3y+2)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb384a4b-9a06-5ed5-bfb1-8be217c6cefa" :answer/id #uuid "eb384a4b-9a06-5ed5-bfb1-8be217c6cefa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(3y-2)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dfb517d-314f-5d0e-adde-8ffca8af7876" :answer/id #uuid "4dfb517d-314f-5d0e-adde-8ffca8af7876" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2x-3)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0fd75856-9255-5679-88e6-6aeda24c9a6c" :question/id #uuid "0fd75856-9255-5679-88e6-6aeda24c9a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109288" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0)$ and $(-1,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/eac7a442f0a794db0606b95aa722320d099f2bcc4e530fc6b62e9be889335da2.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}-2xy$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y-2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x-(2y-2x)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-546404cf-f1c6-53f4-be84-033edffb255a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-546404cf-f1c6-53f4-be84-033edffb255a" :answer-field/id #uuid "546404cf-f1c6-53f4-be84-033edffb255a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f479d213-1c2b-52df-b690-7c60433780fa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-97cc6eca-3853-56fc-b7f2-30027a479f07" "answer-f479d213-1c2b-52df-b690-7c60433780fa" "answer-268fff15-9ff4-545d-b493-583e83e9bc8d" "answer-bb803066-80a1-53e9-86c1-b29e905214f6" "answer-9947a74c-b42b-54c1-b91a-071c2ba392f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97cc6eca-3853-56fc-b7f2-30027a479f07" :answer/id #uuid "97cc6eca-3853-56fc-b7f2-30027a479f07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f479d213-1c2b-52df-b690-7c60433780fa" :answer/id #uuid "f479d213-1c2b-52df-b690-7c60433780fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-268fff15-9ff4-545d-b493-583e83e9bc8d" :answer/id #uuid "268fff15-9ff4-545d-b493-583e83e9bc8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb803066-80a1-53e9-86c1-b29e905214f6" :answer/id #uuid "bb803066-80a1-53e9-86c1-b29e905214f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{1+x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9947a74c-b42b-54c1-b91a-071c2ba392f9" :answer/id #uuid "9947a74c-b42b-54c1-b91a-071c2ba392f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{1+x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c14fef09-0118-528c-b9c9-91bf74ebf7eb" :question/id #uuid "c14fef09-0118-528c-b9c9-91bf74ebf7eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109245" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-2,0),(1,0),(1,1)$ and $(-2,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x{y}^{2})\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a5404053d83188af931c19350d5b6e42145009cf7f67b9dffcc08edc16ad3627.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-2\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0551862e-7b6d-55d9-92c8-f74ab0aa1c10"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0551862e-7b6d-55d9-92c8-f74ab0aa1c10" :answer-field/id #uuid "0551862e-7b6d-55d9-92c8-f74ab0aa1c10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6622f567-4548-58c5-9664-16e371447a8c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-50d8a1be-eef6-58bc-8c04-9f0c7278b337" "answer-1f28acf4-a8d0-5ea3-bf43-bdd1494c0d0d" "answer-6622f567-4548-58c5-9664-16e371447a8c" "answer-05110615-25ea-51a7-9ec6-fe0602b9d168" "answer-d3af5033-176c-54c5-aa13-e691e816dd84"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50d8a1be-eef6-58bc-8c04-9f0c7278b337" :answer/id #uuid "50d8a1be-eef6-58bc-8c04-9f0c7278b337" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f28acf4-a8d0-5ea3-bf43-bdd1494c0d0d" :answer/id #uuid "1f28acf4-a8d0-5ea3-bf43-bdd1494c0d0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6622f567-4548-58c5-9664-16e371447a8c" :answer/id #uuid "6622f567-4548-58c5-9664-16e371447a8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05110615-25ea-51a7-9ec6-fe0602b9d168" :answer/id #uuid "05110615-25ea-51a7-9ec6-fe0602b9d168" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(y+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3af5033-176c-54c5-aa13-e691e816dd84" :answer/id #uuid "d3af5033-176c-54c5-aa13-e691e816dd84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5bd170e0-be10-5a08-9034-fb1371f7f685" :question/id #uuid "5bd170e0-be10-5a08-9034-fb1371f7f685" :question/math-academy-id "q-41475" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}-xy+11,{y}^{2}+1⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/007634ca34871f05cde9d44a58fcf24b1e5fd3b8c337c68f2025fb8724eef819.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(6{x}^{2}-xy+11)\\,dx+({y}^{2}+1)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6{x}^{2}-xy+11$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}+1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,-\\frac{x}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(6{x}^{2}-xy+11)\\,dx+({y}^{2}+1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[0-(-x)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{x/2}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x[y]_{-x/2}^{x/2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x(x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d3408749-b613-5708-9ee5-7482588129d0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d3408749-b613-5708-9ee5-7482588129d0" :answer-field/id #uuid "d3408749-b613-5708-9ee5-7482588129d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-64f04a8e-60e7-5cb7-93ee-ee43e5f4bcee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5bd1f5ef-c9ed-5994-83b0-1b7bfde99771" "answer-f37da209-7b24-5d10-b640-c3d7580c8d8b" "answer-8b0a07cf-1f8b-5b69-8fec-824801c47fec" "answer-fd217b62-f697-5f47-89d1-aa9d6c12c868" "answer-64f04a8e-60e7-5cb7-93ee-ee43e5f4bcee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bd1f5ef-c9ed-5994-83b0-1b7bfde99771" :answer/id #uuid "5bd1f5ef-c9ed-5994-83b0-1b7bfde99771" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f37da209-7b24-5d10-b640-c3d7580c8d8b" :answer/id #uuid "f37da209-7b24-5d10-b640-c3d7580c8d8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b0a07cf-1f8b-5b69-8fec-824801c47fec" :answer/id #uuid "8b0a07cf-1f8b-5b69-8fec-824801c47fec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd217b62-f697-5f47-89d1-aa9d6c12c868" :answer/id #uuid "fd217b62-f697-5f47-89d1-aa9d6c12c868" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64f04a8e-60e7-5cb7-93ee-ee43e5f4bcee" :answer/id #uuid "64f04a8e-60e7-5cb7-93ee-ee43e5f4bcee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6fbdc55e-ae46-5858-9840-dcfa0a36807f" :question/id #uuid "6fbdc55e-ae46-5858-9840-dcfa0a36807f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109149" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}+y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the rectangle with vertices at $(-1,0),$ $(3,0),$ $(3,2)$ and $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/39/3954846460076fa1ef1e5826d32bde9145e7350cb2324072b001d8c6ff41cc14.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=xy$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}+y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4x-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}3x[y]_{0}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}3x(2)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}6x\\,dx \\\\  & =3[{x}^{2}]_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =3[9-1] \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c9adf5ad-146a-5b8c-a73a-531cc3071103"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c9adf5ad-146a-5b8c-a73a-531cc3071103" :answer-field/id #uuid "c9adf5ad-146a-5b8c-a73a-531cc3071103" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8a4d1306-f937-5ce3-9c78-97d29184dca0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-008b0a85-ca8c-5ca9-bac7-3b09c846bdfc" "answer-cac62874-d5b4-593b-8361-008219384519" "answer-1752e0da-3afa-549b-a738-e3248a7caa51" "answer-8a4d1306-f937-5ce3-9c78-97d29184dca0" "answer-a73d50e9-baec-56fe-b31f-f9d4aaf5eb2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-008b0a85-ca8c-5ca9-bac7-3b09c846bdfc" :answer/id #uuid "008b0a85-ca8c-5ca9-bac7-3b09c846bdfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cac62874-d5b4-593b-8361-008219384519" :answer/id #uuid "cac62874-d5b4-593b-8361-008219384519" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{31}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1752e0da-3afa-549b-a738-e3248a7caa51" :answer/id #uuid "1752e0da-3afa-549b-a738-e3248a7caa51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a4d1306-f937-5ce3-9c78-97d29184dca0" :answer/id #uuid "8a4d1306-f937-5ce3-9c78-97d29184dca0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a73d50e9-baec-56fe-b31f-f9d4aaf5eb2c" :answer/id #uuid "a73d50e9-baec-56fe-b31f-f9d4aaf5eb2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134367 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,{x}^{2}-2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le \\pi ,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,$ traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f75ee554c61dc799273c2c731d608766d27987937d0058df6ee1e0290d8b060.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}-2y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}-2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\pi ,0\\le y\\le \\pi }\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}-2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{[2xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]}_{0}^{\\pi }\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\pi x+2)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\pi {x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{3}+2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b60e1b91-b85b-56e9-90f2-353a69264f24"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b60e1b91-b85b-56e9-90f2-353a69264f24" :answer-field/id #uuid "b60e1b91-b85b-56e9-90f2-353a69264f24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-950ad376-e54c-57af-bfa1-45e819b52db4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c5551b46-21d7-59b0-b72c-4198c2a76d8e" "answer-950ad376-e54c-57af-bfa1-45e819b52db4" "answer-ab82bc31-acab-5197-b9ab-1bdf4558c405" "answer-d84a9c83-a85a-5496-be1f-846504032a6f" "answer-7924d408-b0a6-57f4-9535-29236f3d016b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5551b46-21d7-59b0-b72c-4198c2a76d8e" :answer/id #uuid "c5551b46-21d7-59b0-b72c-4198c2a76d8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-950ad376-e54c-57af-bfa1-45e819b52db4" :answer/id #uuid "950ad376-e54c-57af-bfa1-45e819b52db4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}+2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab82bc31-acab-5197-b9ab-1bdf4558c405" :answer/id #uuid "ab82bc31-acab-5197-b9ab-1bdf4558c405" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d84a9c83-a85a-5496-be1f-846504032a6f" :answer/id #uuid "d84a9c83-a85a-5496-be1f-846504032a6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7924d408-b0a6-57f4-9535-29236f3d016b" :answer/id #uuid "7924d408-b0a6-57f4-9535-29236f3d016b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c2f5a35-b3df-565f-bebb-8a3475dfd0d6" :question/id #uuid "8c2f5a35-b3df-565f-bebb-8a3475dfd0d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-109144" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2xy-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2x)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(-1,0),$ $(1,0)$ and $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb85e51584f0c41fc19210a5ce56289baa652aff3046d5d88c034d187b7e45dd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2xy-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2xy-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2-2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(2+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2y+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2(1-x)+(1-x)^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-4x+3)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2}+3x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-2+3]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-2-3] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6dc55032-e087-5612-84b6-a80c771dff9e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6dc55032-e087-5612-84b6-a80c771dff9e" :answer-field/id #uuid "6dc55032-e087-5612-84b6-a80c771dff9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c3e38dea-a3e8-5c30-a757-acb922de2e15" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b224effd-33ce-5fb0-9db8-2bd01771711f" "answer-61c02992-1335-5c6e-a197-06ab323b3dc4" "answer-b4dfbed0-cc00-5564-812f-23a1d3638249" "answer-2bb0f3ad-fee3-562b-9e95-4b5539d42793" "answer-c3e38dea-a3e8-5c30-a757-acb922de2e15"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b224effd-33ce-5fb0-9db8-2bd01771711f" :answer/id #uuid "b224effd-33ce-5fb0-9db8-2bd01771711f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61c02992-1335-5c6e-a197-06ab323b3dc4" :answer/id #uuid "61c02992-1335-5c6e-a197-06ab323b3dc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4dfbed0-cc00-5564-812f-23a1d3638249" :answer/id #uuid "b4dfbed0-cc00-5564-812f-23a1d3638249" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bb0f3ad-fee3-562b-9e95-4b5539d42793" :answer/id #uuid "2bb0f3ad-fee3-562b-9e95-4b5539d42793" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3e38dea-a3e8-5c30-a757-acb922de2e15" :answer/id #uuid "c3e38dea-a3e8-5c30-a757-acb922de2e15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4383616d-c261-5116-9385-9c929fedb95e" :question/id #uuid "4383616d-c261-5116-9385-9c929fedb95e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109157" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dx+(x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the trapezoid with vertices at $(0,1),$ $(0,-5),$ $(4,-1)$ and $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/acd7ae0cc90007b9f710f242ab1f0498b2439cdbb8951df388b9d8d0588d553f.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dx+(x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =1+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 4,\\,x-5\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dx+(x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{x-5}^{1}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[y]_{x-5}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(6-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[6x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =24-\\frac{16}{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b6016823-928e-5a70-98df-762771d654fe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b6016823-928e-5a70-98df-762771d654fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6016823-928e-5a70-98df-762771d654fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-614e19a5-0156-5aa6-92f4-46816789f3b6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3e7a1a9b-014d-571b-8c35-beddd81764b4" "answer-f51732c9-6bbf-50cf-aa38-918f942e3f4f" "answer-c3cc7632-9b26-5a7b-bf16-9202ce5e54a1" "answer-614e19a5-0156-5aa6-92f4-46816789f3b6" "answer-2973f614-b513-5cf6-8c17-61bf7293f111"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e7a1a9b-014d-571b-8c35-beddd81764b4" :answer/id #uuid "3e7a1a9b-014d-571b-8c35-beddd81764b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f51732c9-6bbf-50cf-aa38-918f942e3f4f" :answer/id #uuid "f51732c9-6bbf-50cf-aa38-918f942e3f4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3cc7632-9b26-5a7b-bf16-9202ce5e54a1" :answer/id #uuid "c3cc7632-9b26-5a7b-bf16-9202ce5e54a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-614e19a5-0156-5aa6-92f4-46816789f3b6" :answer/id #uuid "614e19a5-0156-5aa6-92f4-46816789f3b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2973f614-b513-5cf6-8c17-61bf7293f111" :answer/id #uuid "2973f614-b513-5cf6-8c17-61bf7293f111" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135108 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b].$ The fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,\\textrm d x = f(b) - f(a).\n$$\n\nThis theorem relates the integral of $f'$ over the domain $D = [a,b]$ to the values of $f$ at the boundary of $D.$ Here, the boundary of $D$ consists of the endpoints $x=a$ and $x=b$ only.\n\nAn analogous result, known as **Green's theorem,** exists for double integrals. It states the following:\n\n*Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, and simple closed curve in $\\Bbb R^2,$ and let $D$ be the region enclosed by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ are scalar functions that have continuous partial derivatives, then* \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right) \\: \\textrm{d}A  = \\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nGreen's theorem is seen as an analog of the fundamental theorem of calculus because it relates an integral of the derivatives of $P$ and $Q$ over a domain $D$ to the values of $P$ and $Q$ on the boundary of $D.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135136 :tutorial/content "It's possible to write Green's theorem in a form that explicitly features the curl. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's denote $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\big\\langle P(x,y), \\: Q(x,y) \\big\\rangle.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F}(x,y) \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nNow, by thinking of $\\mathbf{F}$ as a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ with zero $z$-component, we can write\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,0) = \\big\\langle P(x,y), \\: Q(x,y), \\: 0 \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,0),$ we obtain\n\n![](images/0c/0c894a5d968de42c57384789a00c9438b96af42e65ce69fc78cf67c18b49a462.svg)\n\nAs a result, we can recover the original scalar curl by taking the dot product of $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ with the vector $\\mathbf k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{curl}\\:\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{k} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial y}\\right) \\mathbf k \\cdot \\mathbf k = \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write Green's theorem in the form\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F}(x,y) \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \n= \\iint\\limits_D {\\text{curl}\\:\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{k}} \\, \\textrm d A.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133997 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
